1
00:00:00,343 --> 00:00:02,339
Last video ended with this question.

2
00:00:02,903 --> 00:00:05,555
The train's waiting-time density is flat from one

3
00:00:05,617 --> 00:00:06,058
to five.

4
00:00:06,723 --> 00:00:07,537
Where does it balance?

5
00:00:08,486 --> 00:00:09,710
Right in the middle, at three,

6
00:00:10,214 --> 00:00:12,061
because the two halves are mirror images.

7
00:00:13,046 --> 00:00:14,914
But most densities aren't flat.

8
00:00:15,599 --> 00:00:17,693
Think of a running playlist on shuffle.

9
00:00:18,299 --> 00:00:20,272
Song lengths run from about two to six

10
00:00:20,313 --> 00:00:20,554
minutes.

11
00:00:21,299 --> 00:00:22,962
How long is a song on average,

12
00:00:23,484 --> 00:00:25,487
and so roughly how many songs fit in

13
00:00:25,508 --> 00:00:26,429
a forty-minute run?

14
00:00:27,492 --> 00:00:29,515
We'll build the method on a simpler density

15
00:00:29,576 --> 00:00:29,856
first:

16
00:00:30,558 --> 00:00:33,042
f of x equals x over eight, from

17
00:00:33,083 --> 00:00:33,903
zero to four.

18
00:00:34,565 --> 00:00:36,549
It's taller on the right, so it should

19
00:00:36,590 --> 00:00:38,374
balance somewhere right of two.

20
00:00:39,138 --> 00:00:39,823
Where exactly?

21
00:00:40,671 --> 00:00:42,020
And how spread out is it?

22
00:00:43,069 --> 00:00:45,078
In a discrete model we added up values

23
00:00:45,160 --> 00:00:46,325
times probabilities.

24
00:00:47,110 --> 00:00:49,138
Here there's no list of values to add.

25
00:00:49,802 --> 00:00:50,608
So let's make a list.

26
00:00:51,072 --> 00:00:52,806
Cut the interval into four strips.

27
00:00:53,552 --> 00:00:55,882
Each strip's area is about f of x

28
00:00:55,943 --> 00:00:57,630
times its width, delta x.

29
00:00:58,274 --> 00:01:00,882
That's roughly the probability that X lands in

30
00:01:00,903 --> 00:01:01,263
the strip.

31
00:01:01,926 --> 00:01:04,236
So treat each strip like a single value

32
00:01:04,337 --> 00:01:06,085
at its middle, with that probability.

33
00:01:06,909 --> 00:01:09,635
Values further out, and with more probability, pull

34
00:01:09,697 --> 00:01:10,538
harder on the beam.

35
00:01:11,341 --> 00:01:12,502
Now it's a discrete model,

36
00:01:13,084 --> 00:01:14,807
and its balance point is the sum of

37
00:01:15,229 --> 00:01:16,891
x times f of x delta x.

38
00:01:17,413 --> 00:01:19,229
That's two point six two five.

39
00:01:19,956 --> 00:01:21,969
With eight strips, it's two point six five

40
00:01:22,011 --> 00:01:22,251
six.

41
00:01:22,654 --> 00:01:24,834
With sixteen, two point six six four.

42
00:01:25,517 --> 00:01:27,943
As the strips get thinner, the sum becomes

43
00:01:27,984 --> 00:01:28,665
an integral.

44
00:01:29,187 --> 00:01:31,592
So the expected value is the integral from

45
00:01:31,813 --> 00:01:33,957
a to b of x times f of

46
00:01:34,018 --> 00:01:34,738
x, dx.

47
00:01:35,329 --> 00:01:36,053
Let's evaluate it.

48
00:01:36,698 --> 00:01:38,868
Multiply x by x over eight, and you

49
00:01:38,909 --> 00:01:40,275
get x squared over eight.

50
00:01:40,919 --> 00:01:43,143
Its integral from zero to four is one

51
00:01:43,185 --> 00:01:45,870
eighth of x cubed over three, from zero

52
00:01:45,891 --> 00:01:46,171
to four.

53
00:01:46,833 --> 00:01:50,018
That's sixty-four over twenty-four, which is eight thirds,

54
00:01:50,440 --> 00:01:51,742
about two point six seven.

55
00:01:52,584 --> 00:01:54,773
The beam levels there, right of the middle,

56
00:01:55,216 --> 00:01:56,923
pulled over by the taller part of the

57
00:01:56,964 --> 00:01:57,325
density.

58
00:01:58,028 --> 00:01:59,792
And the strip sums were closing in on

59
00:01:59,833 --> 00:02:00,955
exactly this number.

60
00:02:01,999 --> 00:02:03,225
You've met this density before.

61
00:02:03,789 --> 00:02:05,532
It's the cookie from two videos ago,

62
00:02:06,174 --> 00:02:08,418
so the average chip sits about two point

63
00:02:08,479 --> 00:02:11,123
six seven centimetres from the centre, a third

64
00:02:11,184 --> 00:02:11,764
kind of middle,

65
00:02:12,306 --> 00:02:14,430
just inside the median of two point eight

66
00:02:14,471 --> 00:02:14,710
three.

67
00:02:15,674 --> 00:02:18,129
Like a discrete mean, it's also the long-run

68
00:02:18,311 --> 00:02:18,632
average.

69
00:02:19,136 --> 00:02:21,541
Take many values of X, and their average

70
00:02:21,862 --> 00:02:23,265
settles near eight thirds.

71
00:02:23,961 --> 00:02:26,140
Does this agree with symmetry?

72
00:02:26,923 --> 00:02:27,605
For the train,

73
00:02:28,328 --> 00:02:30,413
f is a quarter from one to five.

74
00:02:30,956 --> 00:02:33,245
So we integrate x over four, which gives

75
00:02:33,406 --> 00:02:35,756
x squared over eight, from one to five.

76
00:02:36,398 --> 00:02:39,674
That's twenty-five minus one, over eight, which is

77
00:02:39,815 --> 00:02:40,116
three,

78
00:02:40,639 --> 00:02:40,920
the middle.

79
00:02:41,664 --> 00:02:44,028
The integral and the symmetry agree, so the

80
00:02:44,069 --> 00:02:45,933
formula passes a test we can check by

81
00:02:46,114 --> 00:02:46,193
eye.

82
00:02:46,936 --> 00:02:49,457
For any uniform distribution from a to b,

83
00:02:49,798 --> 00:02:52,019
the mean is a plus b over two,

84
00:02:52,540 --> 00:02:53,020
the midpoint.

85
00:02:53,729 --> 00:02:54,493
Now, spread.

86
00:02:55,198 --> 00:02:58,186
The variance is still the expected squared distance

87
00:02:58,247 --> 00:02:58,707
from mu.

88
00:02:59,370 --> 00:03:01,053
So we weight each squared distance,

89
00:03:01,575 --> 00:03:04,019
x minus mu, squared, by f of x

90
00:03:04,180 --> 00:03:05,642
dx, and integrate.

91
00:03:06,485 --> 00:03:08,192
And the quicker form carries over too.

92
00:03:08,877 --> 00:03:11,043
Expanding the square works exactly as it did

93
00:03:11,104 --> 00:03:11,905
for a discrete model.

94
00:03:12,588 --> 00:03:14,732
The integral of f is one, and the

95
00:03:14,813 --> 00:03:17,257
integral of x f is mu, just like

96
00:03:17,298 --> 00:03:17,879
the sums were.

97
00:03:18,681 --> 00:03:20,406
That means you don't need to expand a

98
00:03:20,447 --> 00:03:22,313
squared bracket inside the integral.

99
00:03:22,975 --> 00:03:24,638
So the variance is the integral of x

100
00:03:25,060 --> 00:03:27,785
squared f of x, minus mu squared.

101
00:03:28,388 --> 00:03:30,734
For x over eight, E of X squared,

102
00:03:30,935 --> 00:03:32,880
the average of the squared values, is the

103
00:03:33,021 --> 00:03:35,869
integral of x cubed over eight, from zero

104
00:03:35,890 --> 00:03:36,210
to four.

105
00:03:36,632 --> 00:03:38,858
That's one eighth of x to the fourth

106
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:41,567
over four, or two hundred and fifty-six over

107
00:03:41,628 --> 00:03:42,991
thirty-two, which is eight.

108
00:03:43,756 --> 00:03:46,928
Subtract mu squared, sixty-four ninths, and the variance

109
00:03:47,090 --> 00:03:47,913
is eight ninths.

110
00:03:48,680 --> 00:03:49,290
Here's a quick check.

111
00:03:49,817 --> 00:03:52,084
E of X squared, eight, must be at

112
00:03:52,205 --> 00:03:55,034
least mu squared, about seven point one, or

113
00:03:55,055 --> 00:03:56,157
the variance would be negative.

114
00:03:56,821 --> 00:03:58,929
So sigma is the square root of eight

115
00:03:59,031 --> 00:04:00,978
ninths, about zero point nine four.

116
00:04:01,543 --> 00:04:03,821
It measures the spread around eight thirds.

117
00:04:04,644 --> 00:04:06,487
For a uniform from a to b, the

118
00:04:06,528 --> 00:04:09,593
same steps give b minus a, squared, over

119
00:04:09,634 --> 00:04:09,934
twelve.

120
00:04:10,517 --> 00:04:12,903
For the train, that's sixteen over twelve, which

121
00:04:12,964 --> 00:04:13,605
is four thirds.

122
00:04:14,222 --> 00:04:15,167
Now the playlist.

123
00:04:15,770 --> 00:04:18,934
Suppose song lengths, X minutes, have density pi

124
00:04:18,975 --> 00:04:21,778
over eight, times sin of pi times x

125
00:04:21,858 --> 00:04:24,422
minus two, over four, from two to six.

126
00:04:25,144 --> 00:04:25,932
It is a density.

127
00:04:26,376 --> 00:04:28,287
It's never negative there, and its integral from

128
00:04:28,347 --> 00:04:29,433
two to six is one.

129
00:04:30,217 --> 00:04:32,146
But E of X needs the integral of

130
00:04:32,407 --> 00:04:33,814
x times that sine.

131
00:04:34,559 --> 00:04:36,652
No rule in this course integrates x times

132
00:04:36,713 --> 00:04:37,337
a sine function.

133
00:04:37,779 --> 00:04:39,526
So write the set-up in full, with its

134
00:04:39,586 --> 00:04:42,458
limits, and use an online computational application.

135
00:04:43,041 --> 00:04:44,448
Then say that you used one.

136
00:04:45,112 --> 00:04:47,403
The app gives four minutes, which matches the

137
00:04:47,445 --> 00:04:48,369
symmetry of the curve.

138
00:04:49,112 --> 00:04:50,939
For the variance, set up the integral of

139
00:04:50,960 --> 00:04:53,810
x squared f of x, minus mu squared.

140
00:04:54,373 --> 00:04:56,536
The app gives about zero point seven six,

141
00:04:57,057 --> 00:04:59,401
so sigma is about zero point eight seven

142
00:04:59,441 --> 00:05:01,504
minutes, roughly fifty-two seconds.

143
00:05:02,366 --> 00:05:05,032
Shuffle sixty songs, and their running average settles

144
00:05:05,133 --> 00:05:05,754
near four.

145
00:05:06,356 --> 00:05:08,404
So as a rough guide, forty minutes is

146
00:05:08,445 --> 00:05:10,753
about ten songs' worth, and the last one

147
00:05:10,955 --> 00:05:11,556
may not finish.

148
00:05:12,360 --> 00:05:14,507
Show the limits, x f of x or

149
00:05:14,728 --> 00:05:17,075
x squared f of x, the result, and

150
00:05:17,156 --> 00:05:17,999
the tool you used.

151
00:05:18,576 --> 00:05:19,949
Here's a way to remember the switch.

152
00:05:20,676 --> 00:05:22,218
Sum becomes integral,

153
00:05:22,840 --> 00:05:25,082
and P of x becomes f of x

154
00:05:25,303 --> 00:05:25,763
dx.

155
00:05:26,505 --> 00:05:29,249
That turns each discrete formula for E and

156
00:05:29,270 --> 00:05:31,393
the variance into its continuous one.

157
00:05:34,038 --> 00:05:34,441
Your turn.

158
00:05:35,007 --> 00:05:36,972
Find E of X and the variance of

159
00:05:37,113 --> 00:05:38,156
X for this density.

160
00:05:38,760 --> 00:05:39,569
Pause here to answer.

161
00:05:50,235 --> 00:05:52,500
E of X is the integral of x

162
00:05:52,721 --> 00:05:54,946
times two x, which is two x squared.

163
00:05:55,449 --> 00:05:57,587
From zero to one, that's two thirds.

164
00:05:58,452 --> 00:06:00,937
E of X squared is the integral of

165
00:06:01,058 --> 00:06:03,283
two x cubed, which is a half.

166
00:06:03,966 --> 00:06:06,372
So the variance is a half minus four

167
00:06:06,433 --> 00:06:08,016
ninths, which is one eighteenth.

168
00:06:08,821 --> 00:06:11,099
An expected value of one is wrong.

169
00:06:11,562 --> 00:06:14,171
That integrates f by itself, which always gives

170
00:06:14,312 --> 00:06:14,392
one.

171
00:06:16,144 --> 00:06:16,568
Last one.

172
00:06:17,273 --> 00:06:19,343
Write the integrals you would enter into an

173
00:06:19,604 --> 00:06:21,512
online computational application.

174
00:06:22,175 --> 00:06:22,959
Pause here to answer.

175
00:06:33,595 --> 00:06:35,518
E of X is the integral from zero

176
00:06:35,560 --> 00:06:37,985
to pi over two of x cos x,

177
00:06:38,266 --> 00:06:38,706
dx.

178
00:06:39,289 --> 00:06:41,578
The variance is the integral of x squared

179
00:06:41,659 --> 00:06:44,449
cos x over the same limits, minus mu

180
00:06:44,490 --> 00:06:44,790
squared.

181
00:06:45,552 --> 00:06:47,553
The app gives mu as pi over two

182
00:06:47,594 --> 00:06:50,215
minus one, and the variance as pi minus

183
00:06:50,336 --> 00:06:52,757
three, about zero point one four two.

184
00:06:53,762 --> 00:06:56,757
Squaring the whole integrand, x cos x, is

185
00:06:56,838 --> 00:06:57,098
wrong.

186
00:06:57,702 --> 00:07:00,288
Only x is squared, and that set-up even

187
00:07:00,349 --> 00:07:01,592
gives a negative variance.

188
00:07:03,214 --> 00:07:05,715
So the expected value is the integral of

189
00:07:06,016 --> 00:07:08,177
x f of x, and the variance is

190
00:07:08,218 --> 00:07:10,878
the integral of x squared f, minus mu

191
00:07:10,899 --> 00:07:11,219
squared,

192
00:07:11,840 --> 00:07:12,940
by hand or with an app.

193
00:07:13,803 --> 00:07:16,249
Every density so far came with a formula

194
00:07:16,371 --> 00:07:16,931
on an interval.

195
00:07:17,736 --> 00:07:18,887
Next, a new question.

196
00:07:19,615 --> 00:07:22,280
Here are a thousand simulated bags of chips,

197
00:07:22,802 --> 00:07:25,728
each filled towards one hundred and seventy-five grams.

198
00:07:26,370 --> 00:07:28,118
They pile up in a bell around the

199
00:07:28,159 --> 00:07:28,459
target.

200
00:07:29,165 --> 00:07:29,830
Why that shape?

201
00:07:30,357 --> 00:07:31,569
And when can we model it?

202
00:07:34,301 --> 00:07:35,104
Here are the key notes.

203
